s2b8637pzrw","micro_review":"done","cardcount":"2","derive_version":"1.1.0","review_time":"124","review_operator":"associate-jesiemae-lauron@archive.org","review_date":"20240304103222","creator":"Supreme Court of the United States","catalog_number":"95-1025","imagecount":"71","ocr":"tesseract 5.3.0-6-g76ae","ocr_parameters":"-l eng","ocr_module_version":"0.0.21","ocr_detected_script":"Fraktur","ocr_detected_script_conf":"0.4256","ocr_detected_lang":"en","ocr_detected_lang_conf":"1.0000","page_number_confidence":"0","page_number_module_version":"1.0.5","pdf_module_version":"0.0.23","description":"Case name: Dalton v. Little Rock Family Planning Services<br /><br />Opinion filed: 1996-03-19<br /><br />Docket No.: 95-1025<br /><br />Citations:<br />\u2022 134 L. Ed. 2d 115<br />\u2022 116 S. Ct. 1063<br />\u2022 516 U.S. 474<br />\u2022 1996 U.S. LEXIS 1719<br /><br />Case holding summaries:<br />\u2022 \"In a pre-emption case such as this, state law is displaced only `to the extent that it actually conflicts with federal law.'\"<br />\u2022 remanding for the entry of an injunction against the enforcement of State law to the extent that it conflicted with the Medicaid Act<br />\u2022 remanding for the entry of an injunction against the enforcement of State law to the extent that it conflicted with the Medicaid Act<br />\u2022 \"[S]tate law is displaced only to the extent it actually conflicts with federal law.\"<br />\u2022 enjoining provision only to the extent that it imposes obligations inconsistent with federal law<br />\u2022 modifying injunction in similar case to \"tak[e] into account the changeable nature of spending bills in general, and the Hyde Amendment in particular, which in some years are very restrictive and in other years are less so\"","external-identifier":"urn:courtlistener:118006/dalton-v-little-rock-family-planning-services","date":"1996","access-restricted-item":"true","subject":"Decision"},"page_numbers":{
